'''''Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana''''' ({{lang|ca|Vila de Llevant}}) is a village in the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca of [[Tramuntana Province]], [[Balboa]]. With a population of 8,532 (2026 estimate), it is the comarca capital and is situated on the eastern shore of [[Lake Iwarakí]]. The village was relocated to its present site in 1970 from its original location on the former shoreline of the lake, in advance of the construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] dam, which raised the water level and submerged the original settlement. As part of the relocation agreement with the [[Birú people|Birú]] community, the comarca capital was moved to the relocated village and the government committed to providing public sector employment to all original residents and their descendants. The village serves as the administrative centre for the comarca, which encompasses the far eastern highlands of Tramuntana Province along the border with [[Nurra Province|Nurra]].

== Geography ==

Vila de Llevant is situated on the eastern shore of [[Lake Iwarakí]], a reservoir lake formed by the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] dam on the lake's southern outflow. The village occupies a narrow strip of level ground between the lake shore and the steep slopes of the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range|Yalabirga]] range. The surrounding terrain is rugged montane highland, with forested ridges rising above the lake.

The climate is cooler than the coastal lowlands, with a pronounced wet season from May to November. The lake moderates local temperatures, and the surrounding highlands receive substantial rainfall, supporting montane forest vegetation.

== Economy ==

The economy of Vila de Llevant is based primarily on subsistence agriculture, with most residents engaged in small-scale farming on the limited arable land along the lake shore and surrounding highland valleys. The cultivation of maize, beans, root vegetables, and coffee provides the primary means of subsistence for the majority of the population.

The village has a minor tourism sector centred on Lake Iwarakí, attracting visitors drawn by the lake's scenic setting in the highlands and its still, mirror-like surface during the dry season, from which the lake's Birú name (meaning "clear reflections") is derived. Tourism infrastructure remains limited, consisting of a small number of guesthouses and informal catering establishments. The [[Gran Presa del Nord]] and the lake are the principal attractions, though the area's remoteness and limited transport infrastructure constrain visitor numbers.

The village's role as comarca capital provides a significant number of public sector employment positions, a consequence of the 1970 relocation agreement under which the government committed to providing jobs to all original residents and their descendants. The municipal council, comarca administrative offices, health clinic, and primary school provide employment for a substantial portion of the village's population, making the public sector the largest formal employer in Vila de Llevant alongside subsistence agriculture.

== History ==

The area around Vila de Llevant has been inhabited by the [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] for centuries. The original settlement was a Birú village situated on the shoreline of the natural lake, at a lower elevation than the present village. The Birú maintained communities along the lake shore and in the surrounding highland valleys, relying on the lake for fishing and the valley floors for agriculture. The settlement remained small throughout the colonial and republican periods, with limited growth and minimal integration into the broader colonial economy.

In 1970, the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB) and the provincial government ordered the relocation of Vila de Llevant in advance of the construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]], which would raise the water level of the lake and submerge the original settlement site. The relocation order prompted protests and confrontations between the Birú community and government authorities, as the village's original site held cultural and historical significance for the indigenous population. The government ultimately funded the relocation, and the village was moved to its present position on higher ground on the eastern shore of the newly enlarged reservoir.

As part of the relocation agreement, the government committed to moving the comarca capital from [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] to the relocated village and providing public sector employment to all original residents and their descendants. Sant Jorge had served as the comarca capital prior to the relocation. The agreement was a concession to the Birú community in exchange for the loss of their ancestral village site, and it transformed Vila de Llevant from a small Birú settlement into the administrative centre of the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca. The public sector jobs provided under the agreement remain a significant source of formal employment in the village. Despite the concessions, the relocation was a source of lasting grievance among the Birú communities in the comarca and contributed to the broader opposition of indigenous groups to subsequent hydroelectric development in the Yalabirga highlands.

The dam project had limited direct economic impact on Vila de Llevant compared to [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]], which became the primary service centre for the CHB workforce, though the dam and reservoir did contribute to the village's minor tourism sector.

